<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Mexican Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1863 CE – 1867 CE · capital Mexico City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French-backed empire of the Habsburg archduke Maximilian, a monarchical experiment that ended with his execution at Querétaro in 1867.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1863, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> arranged for an Assembly of Notables to offer the crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-of-mexico">Archduke Maximilian</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-nepomuceno-almonte">Juan Nepomuceno Almonte</a> headed a regency until Maximilian and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlota-of-mexico">Carlota</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> arrived in May 1864.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maximilian, a liberal, disappointed his conservative backers by upholding the Reform laws on church property and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>, while seeking to protect indigenous communities. His power depended on French troops fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez's</a> republicans across the country. In 1865 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-decree">Black Decree</a> ordered the execution of captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. The empire was financed by heavy loans, and the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-castle">Chapultepec Castle</a> was lavish.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to withdraw, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> pulled out his army in 1866 to 1867. Maximilian stayed, was besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-queretaro">Querétaro</a> and captured, and was shot with generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-miramon">Miramón</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-mejia">Mejía</a> on 19 June 1867. The republic was restored as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-mexican-empire.jpg" alt="Second Mexican Empire" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Amartin2671 Using some elements from: Keepscases,Sodacan,Sarumo74 Flag of the Second Mexican Empire (1865-1867).svg C... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Second_Mexican_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1863 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mexico City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maximilian I, Juan Nepomuceno Almonte</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mexican Empire (1864-1867), Empire of Maximilian</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=second-mexican-empire" aria-label="Second Mexican Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2001 1461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Mexican Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2321 1717L2321 1717M2321 1717L5054 728M2321 1717L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M5054 728h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2321 1717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mexico City. <a href